<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE wtml>
<wtml lang="en" wtml-version="1.2">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="wtml-version" content="1.2" />
    <title>Helix Dynamics — Orientation Day</title>
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9002-reactor-anomaly.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the player been assigned to the Mjolnir drive test team and received their engineering badge?" />
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9003-late-shift.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the player chosen to skip orientation and sneak into Lab 7 after hours?" />
    <rendering-seed>helix-dynamics-9001-corporate-scifi-industrial-clean-nervous-first-day</rendering-seed>
  </head>
  <body>
    <atmosphere>
      The Helix Dynamics orbital fabrication yard smells of ozone, recycled air,
      and something faintly chemical that never quite identifies itself. Fluorescent
      strips run the length of every corridor, slightly too bright. The station hums
      at a frequency you feel in your back teeth. It is enormous — three kilometres
      of pressurised ring around a zero-g assembly core — and it is never entirely
      quiet. Somewhere, always, something is being stress-tested.
    </atmosphere>
    <societal-structure>
      Helix Dynamics is a tier-one interstellar engineering contractor: publicly
      traded, 40,000 employees across twelve stations and four planetary offices.
      The fabrication yard is run by a layered hierarchy — Executive Engineers at
      the top, then Leads, Seniors, Mid-levels, and at the bottom: Associates.
      You are an Associate. The culture is performance-driven but not openly
      hostile. People are too busy to be theatrical about it. Unions exist and are
      quietly respected. There is a canteen on every ring-level and the coffee is
      genuinely good.
    </societal-structure>
    <geography>
      The orientation is held in Block C, Ring 2 — a windowless briefing suite
      with too many chairs and a wall-mounted holo-display running a looping
      corporate welcome reel. A corridor outside leads to the Engineering Pool
      (where new hires are assigned to teams), the canteen, and — if you know
      where to look — a maintenance shaft that runs behind Lab 7.
    </geography>
    <general-description>
      Your first day at Helix Dynamics. You've been hired as a Test Engineer,
      Grade 2 — tasked with qualification testing on propulsion systems. The
      project you've been assigned to exists only as a code name in your onboarding
      documents: MJOLNIR. Colleagues in the orientation cohort are nervous or
      trying not to look it. A Lead Engineer named Vasquez is supposed to be
      collecting you after the briefing, but the briefing ran long and she hasn't
      appeared. A notice on the corridor board reads: "Lab 7 — ACCESS RESTRICTED
      PENDING INCIDENT REVIEW." No further explanation.
    </general-description>
    <npcs>
      Dr. Priya Vasquez: Lead Engineer, propulsion. Mid-40s, compact, moves like
      someone who is always five minutes behind schedule. Direct but not unkind.
      Holds her coffee cup with both hands when she's thinking. She will not waste
      your time. She expects the same courtesy.

      Tomás Engel: Fellow orientation-cohort hire, Test Engineer Grade 1. Nervous,
      over-prepared, has already memorised the station map. Wants to make friends
      but doesn't know how to start. Asks good questions during briefings.

      Station Orientation AI ("SORA"): A soft-spoken voice that answers queries
      via any terminal. Helpful, slightly over-literal. Will answer questions about
      station layout, protocols, and procedures but will not speculate.
    </npcs>
    <user>
      <initial-location>Block C Briefing Suite, Ring 2, Helix Dynamics Orbital Yard</initial-location>
      <initial-state>
        New hire, Test Engineer Grade 2. First day. Orientation just finished.
        Waiting for Lead Engineer Vasquez. Slightly undercaffeinated.
      </initial-state>
      <description>
        Fresh-hired test engineer starting at one of the sector's most prestigious
        propulsion contractors. You tested ion drives at a smaller outfit for three
        years before this — you know your way around a test rig. What you don't
        know yet is what MJOLNIR is, why Lab 7 is locked down, or whether
        Vasquez's lateness is normal or a sign of something.
      </description>
    </user>
    <constraints>
      Tone: grounded sci-fi workplace drama. Think the engineering sections of The
      Expanse crossed with a real aerospace company culture. No action-movie heroics.
      No aliens. The drama is human and technical. Characters swear occasionally
      but professionally. The tech is hard-ish — plasma drives, stress testing,
      telemetry, pressure cycles — not magic. The player's expertise is real and
      should matter. Humour is dry and situational. The station is enormous and
      mostly mundane; wonder comes from scale and engineering, not spectacle.
    </constraints>
  </body>
</wtml>
